[MOSCOW.FullName]
Battle for Moscow

[MOSCOW.Epilogue]
During the battle, the German troops suffered a tangible defeat. As a result of the counteroffensive and the general offensive , they were pushed back 100-250 km. The Tula, Ryazan and Moscow regions, many districts of the Kalinin, Smolensk and Oryol regions were completely liberated. The Barbarossa plan was finally buried and the war turned into a protracted battle between the economies of the Third Reich and the anti-Hitler coalition, and in the military—strategic plan, a long unstable equilibrium was established on the Eastern Front until the summer of 1942, which ultimately gave the Soviet Union a win - the evacuation of industry to the East was almost completely completed and its deployment was proceeding at a rapid pace there.

The victory near Moscow was of great political and military importance. It marked the collapse of Germany's strategic plans, marked the beginning of the deployment of mass resistance in the occupied territories of the USSR, and served to strengthen the anti-Hitler coalition.

At the same time, the Wehrmacht forces were able to maintain the front and the Rzhev-Vyazma bridgehead. The Soviet troops failed to defeat Army Group Center. Thus, the decision on the possession of the strategic initiative was postponed until the summer campaign of 1942.

[MOSCOW.Description]
During the first half of October 1941, Army Group Center, which incorporated the 4th and 9th Armies, the 3rd and 4th Panzer Groups, and the 2nd Panzer Army launched the first all-out offensive toward Moscow. The goal was simple, the annihilation of all Red Army forces in the area and the eventual capture of the capital city, Moscow. Resistance by Red Army units was fierce and the Wehrmacht suffered devastating looses in men and equipment. By the end of October, the offensive was all but spent and while fighting continued, it was merely local actions with no real strategic value. This first major offensive had failed but by the middle of November 1941, the Germans were prepared to try again...
[M001.FullName]
Deed of the Panfilovtsy
[M001.Description]
Under the command of General Konstantin Rokossovsky, the Russian 16th Army was tasked with defending the approaches to Moscow along the German axis of advance. In late November 1941, the Germans attempted to orchestrate a series of converging strikes along the 16th Armies Northern Flank in order encircle and destroy them as well as preventing any remnants from retreating back towards the Moscow defensive ring. This second German offensive on Moscow came from two directions. The first approach was towards Klin following the Rogochevo-Dmitrov line. The second approach came from the area of Solnechnogorsk along the Oktyabrskaya railroad and the Moscow-Leningrad highway.
[M002.FullName]
Critical Line
[M002.Description]
Relentless German attacks have forced the 30th and 16th Armies to gradually fall back to new defensive positions day by day. By mid November however, the Germans had refocused their attention away from Chismena and instead concentrated on Tarasovo, Rumyantsevo and Onufriyevo as they tried to take Istra Town.
[M003.FullName]
Steel Shield
[M003.Description]
Even with the formation and deployment of three Soviet Shock Armies, the Germans offensive toward Moscow continues and even now the city is almost within sight of their advanced units. The Red Army is forced to constantly retreat and isolated under-strength units hold entire sections of the front. This situation has permitted frequent German breakthroughs into the vulnerable Russian rear areas.
[M004.FullName]
Frontline
[M004.Description]
By November 22, the 16th Army had retreated all the way back to the River Istra and the Istra Reservoir. The terrain here was ideal for a steady defense so once more, the brave troops of the Red Army dug in and awaited the oncoming German attack.
On November 24, the German units approached the Istra River and found the 16th Army units deployed all along eastern shore line of the reservoirs. The Germans now began to prepare to cross this heavily defended river.
November 26-27th, the Germans cross the Istra River and attack along the seam between the extreme left flank of the 16th Army (occupied by the 9th and 18th Guards Rifle Divisions) and the right flank of the 5th Armies 108th Rifle Division.
[M005.FullName]
Approaches to Moscow
[M005.Description]
Bolstered by their victories so far, on December 1st, 1941 the Germans launched what they hoped would be the final phase on their campaign to take Moscow. Employing their 4th Army, the Germans intended to break through the Soviet defenses in and around Zvenigorod and Naro-Fominsk and then launch a series of converging attacks towards Kubinka and Golitsino, effectively encircling the 5th and 33rd Armies. Once that was accomplished, the approach to Moscow along the Kiev-Moscow and Minsk-Moscow highways would be clear.
[M006.FullName]
For Motherland
[M006.Description]
During the chilly afternoon of November 30th, the Soviet 16th Army launched a counter-offensive all along their front lines. The villages of Kryukovo and Peshki saw the heaviest fighting of the day. The Germans had built up their defenses around Kryukovo and the village itself changed hands 8 times. Neither side was willing to give any quarter and the ground was littered with the dead and dying and the burned out remains of machine and weaponry. In the end, General Konstantin Rokossovsky and his 16th Army were able to stop the Germans advance towards Moscow, for the moment at least.
[M007.FullName]
Counter-Offensive
[M007.Description]
The momentum had now changed and the 16th Army began to pursue the Germans as they retreated back towards the Istra River. Two new Shock Groups were formed to deal with this new strategic situation. The first Shock Group was tasked to strike near Zhilino, Maryino and Sokolovo and was comprised of the 145 Tank Brigade, the 44th Cavalry Division and 17th Rifle Brigade. The second Shock Group was made up of the 9th Guards Rifle Division and the 17th, 36th, 40th and 89th Rifle Brigades and was designated to strike Istra City itself and further north. On the cold morning of December 10th, both Shock Groups attacked.
[M008.FullName]
Flank Attack
[M008.Description]
Close coordination between the 5th and 16th Armies was a crucial component to victory as both massive bodies of men and machine advanced westward while the German units continued to be pushed back. By December 15th, the Red Army forces had smashed the German defenses along the Istra Reservoir and prepared for an attack towards Volokolamsk. December 16th found the advance Russian units along a line that stretched through Novopetrovskoye – Rumyantsevo – Yadromino and Novo-Daryino. An impressive achievement and change of fortune when only weeks before it seemed the very war was at a loss.
[M009.FullName]
Chasing Enemy
[M009.Description]
The fighting in December was along a rapidly changing front and was now distinguished by the constant pursuit of retreating German troops. The commander of the 20th Army, focused his main thrust at re-capturing the town of Volokolamsk and then, with the aid of the 1St Shock Army and the 16th Army he intended to position his troops along a new front line running from Shakhovskaya though to Andreyevskoye and finally into Chernevo.
The key component to this strategy was taking back the town of Volokolamsk and that task was handed to General Remizov. Under his command, Remizov wielded the 131st and 145th Tank Divisions and the 17th and 24th Tank Brigades. The attack was launched with vigor but by the end of December 17th, the German 106th Infantry Division and the 2nd and 5th Panzer Divisions defending the town had held out and the attack failed.
Frustrated, but not yet defeated, General Remizov launched another attack on the 18th toward Chismena. This attack was supported with the help of the 1st Guards, the 17th Tank Brigade and the 89th Tank Battalion all under the command of General Katukov.
[M010.FullName]
Standoff
[M010.Description]
December 18th began with the Germans still in possession of Volokolamsk and outlying areas. Defending units now included the remnants of the SS "Das Reich" Division as well as the 252nd Infantry Division and the 10th Panzer Division. Again the 16th Army, working in tandem with the 20th Army attempted to destroy the German defenders and on December 19th, Generals Remizov and Katukov again launched a renewed attack on the town. General Remizov's forces, along with the 64th Rifle Brigade occupied the villages of Pushkari and Chentsy and pushed ahead towards Volokolamsk from the North and North-East. The German 106th and 5th Panzer Divisions offered stiff resistance though and by nightfall of the 19th, General Katukov was engaged near Yadrovo and Yazvische and the town of Volokolamsk was still in German hands.